WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.390
Schema validation gives teams a repeatable way to describe runtime expectations.

2
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:11.089
In TypeScript projects, common choices include Zod, Joi, Yup, and Ajv with JSON Schema.

3
00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:19.405
The best choice depends on the application, the ecosystem, and whether schemas need to be shared with non-TypeScript services.

4
00:00:19.405 --> 00:00:30.725
The important engineering decision is not the brand of library; it is having a consistent validation pattern that is easy to review, test, and use at every important boundary.

5
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:34.421
Good schema use is more than checking that a field exists.

6
00:00:34.421 --> 00:00:37.579
It should distinguish parsing from validating.

7
00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:43.431
Parsing takes an unknown input and returns either a known, typed result or a clear failure.

8
00:00:43.431 --> 00:00:55.367
Along the way, the schema may normalize values, apply defaults, reject extra properties, coerce carefully chosen primitives, or preserve strictness where coercion would be unsafe.

9
00:00:55.367 --> 00:01:02.452
The output should be a shape that downstream code can trust without reinterpreting the original raw payload.

10
00:01:02.452 --> 00:01:09.998
One advantage of TypeScript-oriented schema tools is that they can infer compile-time types from runtime schemas.

11
00:01:09.998 --> 00:01:18.546
That alignment reduces duplicate definitions and helps prevent the type from promising something the validator does not enforce.

12
00:01:18.546 --> 00:01:24.321
Shared schemas can also help client and server code agree on request and response shapes.

13
00:01:24.321 --> 00:01:32.638
Even then, the server must validate independently because client code can be bypassed, modified, or pinned to an older version.

14
00:01:32.638 --> 00:01:35.179
Schema drift is the quiet failure mode.

15
00:01:35.179 --> 00:01:43.803
It happens when TypeScript interfaces, runtime validators, API documentation, tests, and business expectations move out of sync.

16
00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:58.435
Teams reduce drift by placing schemas close to the boundary they protect, deriving types from schemas when practical, reviewing validation changes with API changes, and testing invalid inputs as deliberately as valid ones.

17
00:01:58.435 --> 00:02:02.747
The goal is a single defensible path from raw input to trusted data.
